Id: pam003w
Severity: WARN
Category: accounts
Check: check_pam
References: pam_faillock(8), faillock.conf(5)
Controls: CIS 4.1; NIST AC-7; ISO 8.5; CE User access control

No PAM module locks an account after repeated failed logins: neither
pam_faillock nor the older pam_tally2 is in an auth stack. Passwords
can then be guessed at whatever rate the service allows, for as long
as it takes. (SSH logins with keys alone are not affected.)

To fix it, use pam_faillock: 'authselect enable-feature with-faillock'
on Fedora and RHEL; elsewhere, pam_faillock.so preauth and authfail
lines around pam_unix in the auth stack (common-auth on Debian and
Ubuntu), as pam_faillock(8) shows. deny=5 and unlock_time=900 in
/etc/security/faillock.conf are common settings. Locking can be used to
lock users out on purpose, which is why root is exempt by default.
